/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An AC line has series inductance and shunt capacitance.
   - A DC line carries only load current; the loss is I²R.
   - DC also separates the two grids.
   - The classic station uses thyristors in a 12-pulse bridge, two of the three-phase controlled rectifiers met earlier in the subject.
   - Reactive power: a line-commutated bridge absorbs reactive power at both ends.
   - Commutation failure: the inverter valve that is turning off must be given time, the extinction angle γ, to regain its blocking ability.
   - A voltage-source converter station uses IGBT bridges with PWM, like the inverters of the DC-AC inverters topic.
   - Pd = Vd × Id
   - Pd is the total DC power in watts; Vd is the DC voltage in volts and Id the line current in amperes.
   - Arrangements.
*/
