/* book exam traps: Putting the pole-to-ground voltage into Pd = Vd Id for a bipolar link. The formula uses the pole-to-pole voltage, twice the pole-to-ground value, with the current of one pole. | Assuming the inverter generates reactive power while the rectifier absorbs it. Both line-commutated stations absorb reactive power, roughly half the DC power each; the filters only supply part of it. | Treating a commutation failure as a permanent fault. It is a temporary short circuit across the DC terminal, and the link recovers within a few cycles after the AC voltage returns. | Swapping the reversal mechanisms: an LCC reverses DC voltage polarity at constant current direction, while a VSC reverses current direction at constant voltage polarity. | Believing an HVDC line has charging current or a reactive voltage drop. It has neither, and that is exactly why DC suits long overhead routes and cables. */
